1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
The government knows better, or at least claims to know better.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,600
Isn't it odd for the government to go and try

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,560
to fix an information problem when it doesn't know any better

4
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:11,880
than anybody else?

5
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,740
Now, if this is true, so if it's true

6
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:15,320
that the problem is simply that there's

7
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:17,120
asymmetric information but the government has

8
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:22,240
extra information, then there's a really simple, light-handed

9
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:24,000
intervention government can do.

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,880
And this is publicly reveal what it knows.

11
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:32,080
So the government can solve the asymmetric information problem

12
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:35,440
by giving away its information.

13
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:37,320
And you say, who would believe the government?

14
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:38,700
Well, if the government is really

15
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:42,320
so noble in its efforts and so vast in its understanding,

16
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:44,760
then people would be hanging on the government's every word

17
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:46,320
to go, oh my god, the government says

18
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:47,840
that these cars are a bad deal.

19
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:51,200
I don't want them anymore.

20
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,560
Now, you may notice out of all of the discussion

21
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:55,800
about the opioid epidemic, what is

22
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:00,600
one thing we pretty much never heard the government say?

23
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,480
Any government agents here?

24
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:04,320
Maybe they do it, but probably they actually

25
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:05,480
do it in K through 12.

26
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:07,400
And this is, the government has determined

27
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:08,360
this is really dangerous.

28
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,480
And you don't know this yet, but we're telling you,

29
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,640
it's dangerous.

30
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:13,960
So that could be yours.

31
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,040
What is the solution to the opioid epidemic?

32
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:18,720
Just the president gets on the news and my fellow Americans,

33
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:20,220
the government has looked into this,

34
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:22,440
and we found that these drugs are dangerous.

35
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:25,880
One in 10, more and more are more dangerous than you think.

36
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:28,520
OK, all right, carry on.

37
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:30,640
Problem solved.

38
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:32,040
Just you.

39
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:33,600
Yeah, give them a ceiling.

40
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,480
All right, we solved that.

41
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,080
We've revealed this information.

42
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:39,680
And of course, they could get more detail saying, look,

43
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:42,680
according to our calculations, there's a 3% chance

44
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,080
that if you use these drugs regularly for the next five

45
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:47,200
years, you will die.

46
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:48,720
Just thought you ought to know.

47
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:50,200
I know I'm dorky.

48
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,960
Yeah, we have a question back there.

49
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,520
All right.

50
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:59,360
So in this case, the government reestablishes

51
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:01,720
symmetric information.

52
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:05,520
And so no need to do more.

53
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:08,240
Now, there are plenty of times where government

54
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,920
does go and warn people.

55
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:14,240
Although it's not that common that they stop at that.

56
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:17,320
But this is just, we're just sharing this information.

57
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:19,240
So the Surgery General has determined

58
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:21,360
that cigarette smoking is hazardous to your health.

59
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:22,680
Put that warning there.

60
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:25,160
And now you know what the government doesn't know.

61
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:28,800
Now you know what the government knows.

62
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:33,800
All right, so that is simple stage.

63
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:36,720
Now, if you've got asymmetric information,

64
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:39,960
then there's going to be some efficiency implications,

65
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:42,080
as we've seen.

66
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:43,400
But what are they?

67
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:46,560
Well, so even when market outcomes are inefficient,

68
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:48,320
government may be unable to prove matters.

69
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:49,560
So moral hazard.

70
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:53,120
Here there is a general problem with asymmetric information.

71
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,600
And you do get inefficient results with moral hazard.

72
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:57,040
But what exactly is the government

73
00:02:57,040 --> 00:02:59,120
supposed to do about it?

74
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:02,640
What's the government supposed to do about it?

75
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:04,720
If you get creative, you can think about things.

76
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:07,440
How about jailing people for lying about whether they smoke

77
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:10,320
or they apply for health insurance?

78
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:11,640
Do that.

79
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:12,640
All right, what's that?

80
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:15,800
What moral hazard problem does that solve?

81
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,360
People lying about smoking.

82
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,480
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

83
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:22,360
People don't lie about smoking anymore.

84
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:23,760
Now, just put a warning up there.

85
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:24,840
Now you're going to do it.

86
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:25,880
You're going to reveal it.

87
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:29,600
So you could do that kind of thing.

88
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:30,880
Or let's see.

89
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:32,040
There's other things.

90
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:33,880
So you could actually have the government

91
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:37,280
could go and tax unhealthy behaviors.

92
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:39,740
Now, again, of course, if the government couldn't find out,

93
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:40,880
there'd be a problem.

94
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:42,600
The government could actually mandate

95
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:44,120
that people go and be monitored.

96
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:47,480
Or you could go and use facial recognition technology

97
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,400
and then immediately funnel all the information

98
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:52,560
to insurance companies so they know whether you're lying.

99
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:55,760
This is a really popular idea, right?

100
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:57,480
All right, secure the market failure.

101
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:59,320
We now are going to tell insurance companies

102
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:01,400
everything about you.

103
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:06,600
So again, not very popular, as you might have heard.

104
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:10,840
By the way, so there is not only a popular but also

105
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:14,600
a legal interest in preserving personal privacy.

106
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:17,640
And what is privacy, really?

107
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:19,280
Privacy is asymmetric information,

108
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:22,160
which we've got a bunch of models saying this potentially

109
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:23,480
leads to inefficient results.

110
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:26,480
What's so great about it?

111
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:29,720
And most of the arguments for privacy,

112
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:33,840
the simple arguments anyway, are kind of odd because, well,

113
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:38,160
so we all have a secret to hide, don't we?

114
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:40,720
But who are we hiding it from?

115
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:43,600
Wouldn't that person be better off if they knew?

116
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:46,320
So who's to say that it would be better

117
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:47,640
if everyone keeps their secrets?

118
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:50,880
Maybe better if everyone's secrets were released.

119
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:53,960
What are you trying to hide?

120
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:57,200
So at least we're thinking about.

121
00:04:57,200 --> 00:04:59,760
So you could develop a more complicated economic model,

122
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:01,280
for example.

123
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:03,320
Maybe there's just pure embarrassment.

124
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:05,720
So maybe people are giving you secrets just because they

125
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:06,400
get embarrassed.

126
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:07,480
Other people find out.

127
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:09,920
It's not that you actually are trying

128
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:12,720
to take advantage of anyone else or anything like that.

129
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:14,800
We all would feel better if no one

130
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:17,440
could know what we're thinking all the time.

131
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:18,160
That's possible.

132
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:20,560
But on the other hand, a lot of the secrets people really

133
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:22,520
don't want to be found are the kind of thing

134
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:24,840
that a private investigator would be hired

135
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:26,040
by somebody else to discover.

136
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:34,280
Anyway, so moral hazard is one where usually government just

137
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:35,000
gives up on it.

138
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,320
Or as I said in the case of Obamacare,

139
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:41,880
one obvious effect of Obamacare is to make moral hazard worse.

140
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:44,760
And what are the remedies or anything?

141
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:46,760
So the remedy is live with it.

142
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:48,800
Live with worse incentives.

143
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:52,880
Sounds not really much of a solution at all, is it?

144
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:54,600
Now finally, there are a lot of cases

145
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:56,140
where government can improve matters,

146
00:05:56,140 --> 00:05:59,000
but actual regulations do the opposite.

147
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,080
And this is, again, something worth knowing.

148
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:05,960
So we're on this list.

149
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:08,520
So limiting contractual punishment.

150
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:10,120
So as I said, contractual punishment,

151
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:12,000
this is where you sign a contract where

152
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,320
you say, if I am caught doing something bad,

153
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:15,200
I will pay a fine.

154
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:21,800
And this is actually a way of dealing with moral hazard,

155
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:25,480
first of all, because when you sign that contract,

156
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:26,800
what are you doing to yourself?

157
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:27,840
You're giving yourself a minute.

158
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:29,600
So suppose you sign a contract saying,

159
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:33,960
if you catch me smoking, I owe you $10,000.

160
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:36,640
What are you doing about the incentive to increase

161
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:40,080
your risky behavior if you get the insurance?

162
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:40,580
Curving it.

163
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:42,080
Yeah, you're curving that incentive.

164
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:43,440
Maybe you're getting rid of it.

165
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:45,760
And what advantage is there for the consumer

166
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:48,480
of curving your own opportunism?

167
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:50,240
You're more likely to be sold insurance.

168
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:52,200
Yes, more likely and lower rates.

169
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:54,240
Lower rates.

170
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:56,720
So imagine that you could waive your right to clear bankruptcy

171
00:06:56,720 --> 00:07:00,880
for a loan and then get 1 percentage point off the loan.

172
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:02,880
That could be a good deal.

173
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:05,720
So what do contractual punishment schemes for that?

174
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:07,220
Contractual punishments can be used

175
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:09,000
for dealing with adverse selection

176
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,840
because it is a way for you to convince the seller that you

177
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:15,800
aren't, that you are, in fact, the good kind.

178
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:18,520
Says, look, someone who is good would not actually

179
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:21,680
go and offer me this deal because they

180
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:23,120
would expect to lose from it.

181
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,560
So the very fact they're offering this deal

182
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:26,920
means that this is probably going

183
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:32,280
to be worthwhile to sell the insurance at a low rate.

184
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:33,740
So yeah, so contractual punishments

185
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:37,200
can be used for many purposes.

186
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:40,280
So they're used to some minor extent in labor markets,

187
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:43,480
things like jobs where you have to go and prepay

188
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:46,640
the cost of your uniform or equipment.

189
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:48,480
What happens if you go and you get a job

190
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:51,160
and then you pay for all of your uniform and equipment,

191
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:53,120
especially stuff you can't resell because it's

192
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,760
the uniform and only of that employer, and then they fire

193
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:56,640
you?

194
00:07:56,640 --> 00:07:58,280
So suppose you get fired the day that you

195
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:01,240
start after you shell out a week's worth of income

196
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:02,880
for this job.

197
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:06,040
How do you feel about that?

198
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:07,920
Bad, pretty bad.

199
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:10,800
So this is a reason for you to behave at least well enough

200
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:12,200
to not be fired on your first day.

201
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,040
Now, my econ one teacher, Bill Dickens,

202
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:18,920
had a paper co-authored with a bunch of others

203
00:08:18,920 --> 00:08:22,040
about why employers don't use this to a much greater extent.

204
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:24,560
For example, why is it that in order to get hired,

205
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:28,480
you don't just post a big bond and have employers then

206
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:31,920
seize the money if they're disappointed with you?

207
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:35,440
Now, this is not so different from the old school strategy

208
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:37,440
of say, I'll work for you for free for a month.

209
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:39,060
If you don't like me, no hard feelings.

210
00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:41,200
Just fire me.

211
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:45,520
Now, is that labor contract legal?

212
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:46,520
Maybe.

213
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:48,880
It's a definite maybe on its legality.

214
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:51,880
So isn't zero less than minimum wage?

215
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:52,920
Yes, it is.

216
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:56,680
But there is an exception made for unpaid internships.

217
00:08:56,680 --> 00:08:59,400
And then as to whether you could convincingly

218
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:02,080
argue to a jury that it was, in fact, the case,

219
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:04,440
or if you could just stop it from ever getting to a jury,

220
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:06,440
which is another great strategy.

221
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:08,320
I suppose if it ever got to a jury,

222
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:10,680
you would lose, but it never gets to a jury.

223
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:14,640
That's just as good as being in the right, isn't it?

224
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:15,140
All right.

225
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:16,240
You're into it.

226
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:18,320
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

227
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:18,820
All right.

228
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:22,360
Yes, so by the way, so the Bureau of Labor Statistics

229
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:27,200
has official rules for when unpaid internships are legal.

230
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:29,200
And the official standard is so bizarre.

231
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:31,400
It's almost hard even to say it with a straight face.

232
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:32,560
But here's the standard.

233
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:35,160
You are allowed to hire unpaid interns

234
00:09:35,160 --> 00:09:37,640
as long as the interns provide no immediate benefit

235
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:40,360
to the employer whatsoever.

236
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:43,200
That's the only way in the case of which it is legal.

237
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:46,960
So on my reading, if you ever get your boss a cup of coffee

238
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:51,320
as an intern, then they have to pay minimum wage.

239
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:54,460
Now, yet unpaid internships continue.

240
00:09:54,460 --> 00:09:55,620
They're all over the place.

241
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:57,380
And plenty of cups of coffee are probably

242
00:09:57,380 --> 00:09:59,880
brought to bosses without anything happening.

243
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:01,880
How can this be in the real world?

244
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:05,960
How can it be that the underpaid internships are existing

245
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:10,120
in defiance of the actual regulations,

246
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:13,600
at least the plain language reading of the regulations?

247
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:17,360
Well, how do the laws get enforced in generally America?

248
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:18,640
So are you complaining?

249
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:19,640
Yes, someone complains.

250
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:22,920
So you'd have to have an intern that comes and sues you.

251
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:26,040
And this does not sound like a really winning lawsuit, does it?

252
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:28,640
Because if they only were to intern for you

253
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:32,440
for free for a summer, then what would you even owe them?

254
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:33,920
Minimum wage for a summer?

255
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:36,640
And they've got to get a lawyer for that?

256
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:38,400
So how much is the lawyer going to charge

257
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,320
to go in and do the case?

258
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:42,880
Maybe more than the money.

259
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:44,600
And let's see.

260
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:46,400
And then on top of it, of course, you

261
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:48,480
cannot or are in no way sure of winning.

262
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:50,480
And then there's another little problem,

263
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:53,000
which is that you might be burning

264
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,440
a bunch of bridges in an industry where you want to work.

265
00:10:56,440 --> 00:10:59,800
So even if your employer does not hire you back

266
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:02,720
for a real job after you do the underpaid internship,

267
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:06,560
do you want to have them as an enemy?

268
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:09,360
Is it a good idea to collect enemies in this world?

269
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:11,360
Would you just recommend that you go around

270
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:13,240
looking for enemies?

271
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:14,800
So if you're a professor, you might

272
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:17,440
want to collect some friendly intellectual adversaries.

273
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:20,080
That's not quite the same as enemies.

274
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:24,000
So that's the best story probably for what's going on,

275
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,760
is that even though the law is being broken with impunity

276
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:30,120
all the time, nevertheless, it actually

277
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:32,520
is almost no one's interest to go pursue these cases.

278
00:11:32,520 --> 00:11:34,840
Really, the only way that it would make sense for these

279
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:36,680
cases to happen would be if one lawyer were

280
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:38,720
to do a class action suit where he gets

281
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:41,120
100 disgruntled interns.

282
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:42,800
But they all got to be the same firms.

283
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:44,160
You can't go and sue an industry.

284
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:46,440
You can't sue 10 different firms when you've

285
00:11:46,440 --> 00:11:47,960
got to go and sue them individually.

286
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:51,760
So that's, again, a saving grace.

287
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:55,040
So you've got limiting contractual punishment.

288
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:57,160
Something else, so there's limiting risk-adjusted

289
00:11:57,160 --> 00:11:58,080
premiums.

290
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:00,360
So again, the whole idea of adverse selection insurance

291
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:06,360
is that it is hard for the first people to drop out

292
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:07,480
are the safe people.

293
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:09,640
And so the people that stay around and buy insurance

294
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:12,280
are the high-risk cases.

295
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:13,960
But this would not happen if firms

296
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:18,320
could charge different rates based upon different risks.

297
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:20,640
Now, in a simple adverse selection model,

298
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:25,440
why don't firms just go and tailor the rates to the risks?

299
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:27,400
In a simple adverse selection model, why don't?

300
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:27,680
What's that?

301
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:28,320
Because they don't know the brand.

302
00:12:28,320 --> 00:12:29,020
They don't know.

303
00:12:29,020 --> 00:12:30,760
The insurance company, despite all

304
00:12:30,760 --> 00:12:33,760
of its quantitative commuting power,

305
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:36,640
has no clue at all who dies and who lives.

306
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:41,680
In the real world, of course, insurance companies generally

307
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:45,280
have great quantitative people who know lots about this stuff.

308
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:46,640
So if they were unregulated, they

309
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:50,080
would go and tailor the rates to the risks, which

310
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:52,440
would then mean that healthy people would

311
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:55,280
get insurance for low rates.

312
00:12:55,280 --> 00:12:58,400
And what would happen to sick people?

313
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:03,040
They could still get insurance if they wanted for high rates.

314
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:05,800
And normally, regulation tries to stop this.

315
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:07,720
And not entirely, so usually it's

316
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:10,760
OK to have a little bit of separation.

317
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:13,720
But regulators don't like it if, say,

318
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:15,960
a perfectly healthy 18-year-old can get insurance

319
00:13:15,960 --> 00:13:21,760
for $5 a month, and a 60-year-old with cancer

320
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:26,520
is paying $10,000 premiums.

321
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:29,600
So then this is why there are regulations that go and try

322
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:31,280
to compress the premiums.

323
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:33,680
And a side effect of compressing the premiums

324
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:36,280
is to cause which market failure?

325
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:37,040
Adverse selection.

326
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:39,960
Adverse selection.

327
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:43,240
And again, if you were to go and push people on this,

328
00:13:43,240 --> 00:13:45,040
I think it's fair to say a lot of people

329
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:47,760
just have no clue what they're doing.

330
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:49,800
And adverse selection, in their minds,

331
00:13:49,800 --> 00:13:52,560
is really just a code word for what it is they really

332
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:57,160
want to do, which is to have low-risk people subsidize

333
00:13:57,160 --> 00:13:59,120
high-risk people.

334
00:13:59,120 --> 00:14:00,880
But again, if people were being more honest,

335
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:02,360
they would say, well, sure.

336
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:04,520
There's no market failure at all.

337
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:06,720
Maybe insurance companies are great at figuring out

338
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:08,840
who's sick and who's healthy and charging everyone

339
00:14:08,840 --> 00:14:13,600
an actually fair premium plus operating expenses.

340
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:15,800
But I don't like that outcome.

341
00:14:15,800 --> 00:14:17,480
I don't want an outcome that's efficient.

342
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:19,040
I want an outcome that's fair.

343
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:21,520
And a fair outcome is for the healthy people

344
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:22,600
to subsidize sick people.

345
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:28,000
Now, again, of course, if this is a case where the sick people

346
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,240
are little children with leukemia,

347
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:32,440
it's very easy to understand the appeal.

348
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:36,000
When, as in a lot of states, the high-risk people

349
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,000
are people who have gotten into multiple bad accidents

350
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,160
and are still on the roads, then it's

351
00:14:41,160 --> 00:14:43,840
very hard to understand what people are thinking.

352
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:47,320
Other than my dad's very well-thought-out position

353
00:14:47,320 --> 00:14:50,640
of, Brian, people need to drive.

354
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:51,800
People need to drive.

355
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:54,920
It is a requirement of human life to drive.

356
00:14:54,920 --> 00:14:58,600
And if insurance companies can charge really high-risk people

357
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:02,360
really high rates, they won't be able to afford driving.

358
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:04,240
An approved version of this would be, well,

359
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:05,320
those people drive anyway.

360
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:07,280
But then it's like, well, you don't

361
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:10,360
take that excuse from people who lose their licenses altogether.

362
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:17,080
So why take that excuse from people who could have gotten,

363
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:19,520
were legal to drive, but they drove without insurance

364
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:22,000
even though they were really high-risk?

365
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:23,120
So anyway, you got that one.

366
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:26,400
And then last one that I have a whole book on

367
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:29,600
is subsidizing education.

368
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:32,280
So if you believe that there's much to the signaling

369
00:15:32,280 --> 00:15:34,320
model of education, this means that there

370
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:36,280
is an inefficiently high level of education

371
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:38,920
because one of the main reasons to go to school

372
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:41,440
is to show off and impress employers.

373
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:44,640
But if everyone shows off, if everyone impresses employers,

374
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:46,280
this just raises the expectations

375
00:15:46,280 --> 00:15:48,080
for how much education you have to have,

376
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:51,000
causing credential inflation, where

377
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,040
the amount of education that you need

378
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:54,880
is higher than for one and the same job

379
00:15:54,880 --> 00:15:57,800
or is higher than the past.

380
00:15:57,800 --> 00:15:59,560
So economist Richard Vedder has this joke

381
00:15:59,560 --> 00:16:02,120
about a future where to get a job as a janitor,

382
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:04,640
you need a master's in janitorial science.

383
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:06,520
A reader from Korea actually sent me

384
00:16:06,520 --> 00:16:08,920
what appears to be a genuine news article

385
00:16:08,920 --> 00:16:12,320
about how a third of the applicants

386
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:15,680
for a government janitorial job in South Korea

387
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:18,280
were college graduates.

388
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:20,520
And now the people hired three have bachelor's degrees

389
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:23,320
and one had an associate's degree.

390
00:16:23,320 --> 00:16:26,840
So it's not actually a master's in janitorial science,

391
00:16:26,840 --> 00:16:28,960
just a bachelor's degree in janitorial science.

392
00:16:28,960 --> 00:16:30,360
Or of course, they don't really have

393
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:31,720
bachelor's degrees in janitorial science.

394
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:34,000
They probably have a bachelor's degree

395
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,760
in, say, Korean literature.

396
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:38,520
And then they wind up competing very fiercely

397
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:41,160
to get a job as a janitor.

398
00:16:41,160 --> 00:16:43,040
Great system, right?

399
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:46,000
Sounds like a path to a successful society

400
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,160
is to have all of your janitors spending 17 years in school.

401
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:56,080
So that is another example of what I'm talking about,

402
00:16:56,080 --> 00:16:58,040
where in a single signaling model,

403
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:00,040
there's already a general tendency

404
00:17:00,040 --> 00:17:01,560
to excessive education.

405
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:03,960
But then what does government come along and do?

406
00:17:03,960 --> 00:17:06,280
Subsidize it to make the problem worse,

407
00:17:06,280 --> 00:17:08,760
which is also known as pouring gasoline on the fire.

408
00:17:11,440 --> 00:17:12,920
So questions about any of this?

409
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:20,720
All this means you can often admit

410
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:23,320
that a severe market failure exists

411
00:17:23,320 --> 00:17:27,480
and then still blame the government for making it worse.

412
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:30,080
Because the fact that government could improve the situation

413
00:17:30,080 --> 00:17:31,400
does not mean that it does.

414
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:32,560
You've got to look at the world and see

415
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:34,080
what government is really doing.

416
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:36,200
And just like in the old Soviet Union,

417
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:38,400
there was an enormous amount of pollution.

418
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:39,960
There were socialists who said, look,

419
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:41,840
as long as government is running the economy,

420
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:44,920
then they will optimally trade off the cost and benefits

421
00:17:44,920 --> 00:17:47,240
pollution so there won't be any pollution problem.

422
00:17:47,240 --> 00:17:49,680
But the Soviet Union seemed to have a horrible problem.

423
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:52,080
And it looked like even though the government was running

424
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:53,800
the industries, they seemed more focused

425
00:17:53,800 --> 00:17:56,840
on making a lot of tanks this year than about cancer

426
00:17:56,840 --> 00:17:57,640
10 years from now.

427
00:17:57,640 --> 00:18:00,120
All right.

428
00:18:00,120 --> 00:18:03,040
OK, so everyone happy?

429
00:18:03,040 --> 00:18:04,920
Or suitably peeved?

430
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:07,880
Or inspired to go and carry out a great research

431
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:10,760
program to expose the horrors of the system?

432
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:13,840
All right, there's all sorts of possible reactions.

433
00:18:13,840 --> 00:18:16,360
All right, now let's move on.

434
00:18:16,360 --> 00:18:18,240
So up till now, I've occasionally

435
00:18:18,240 --> 00:18:20,200
alluded to the existence of another field called

436
00:18:20,200 --> 00:18:22,880
psychology.

437
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:26,040
Now, when I was in grad school, the word psychology

438
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:29,600
was never pronounced without a sneer or sarcasm.

439
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:31,800
Psych-a-ly-ty.

440
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:35,880
If you just want to be psychologists about this.

441
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:38,640
All right, anyway, I'm not going to do this

442
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:40,840
because there is so much that economists

443
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:42,000
have learned from psychology.

444
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:43,360
There's so much we have learned.

445
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:45,240
But even better, there's still a lot of stuff

446
00:18:45,240 --> 00:18:46,960
that hardly any economists are aware of.

447
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:48,680
And all you need to do to get papers

448
00:18:48,680 --> 00:18:53,040
is to find ways of finding out what is so much

449
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:54,960
something useful psychologists know

450
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:56,920
and then be the first economist to apply it

451
00:18:56,920 --> 00:18:58,460
to something an economist care about.

452
00:18:58,460 --> 00:19:00,480
And then you've got a paper.

453
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:02,680
Now, of course, we're going to be talking about are mostly

454
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:04,140
cases where it's already been done.

455
00:19:04,140 --> 00:19:06,480
But there's so much more out there

456
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:09,480
because psychology is an incredibly

457
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:11,560
diverse and broad field.

458
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:13,040
There are many kinds of psychologists

459
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:15,120
that don't even talk to each other.

460
00:19:15,120 --> 00:19:17,320
So this means that you could just

461
00:19:17,320 --> 00:19:19,120
go and talk to a bunch of different kinds

462
00:19:19,120 --> 00:19:24,000
and likely to go and find something out that you can use.

463
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,000
So let's get started.

464
00:19:28,600 --> 00:19:32,120
Now, most economists just take the basic assumptions

465
00:19:32,120 --> 00:19:35,240
of microeconomics for granted.

466
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:35,920
That's there.

467
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:38,080
That's what we know.

468
00:19:38,080 --> 00:19:40,240
So very rarely do economists empirically

469
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:42,840
test the basic microassumptions.

470
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:47,120
But you might say, well, the elementary micro theory

471
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:49,320
almost seems true by definition.

472
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:52,640
Like the thing where the transitivity,

473
00:19:52,640 --> 00:19:57,520
like if A is preferred to B, B preferred to C,

474
00:19:57,520 --> 00:20:24,880
then A is going to be preferred to C.


